{"data":{"updated_timestamp":"12/11/2026 17:41:30","profile":{"player_id":"73768","last_updated":"12/11/2026 17:41","fullname":"Tuba Hassan","short_display_name":"T Hassan","name":"Tuba Hassan","gender":"F","dob":"18/10/2000","date_of_death":null,"place_birth":"Pakistan","nationality_id":"6","nationality":"Pakistan","nationality_short_code":"PAK","nick_name":null,"player_skill":{"id":"2","text":"Bowler","role":"Bowler"},"batting_style":{"id":"1","text":"Right Handed"},"bowling_style":{"id":"5","text":"Leg break"},"writeup":"<p>Effervescent, aggressive, and brimming with natural flair, Tuba Hassan has brought the rare and hypnotic art of leg-spin bowling back to the forefront of Pakistan women cricket. Born in Lahore on October 18, 2000, she approaches the game with an attacking mindset, delighting in tossing<br />\r\nthe ball above the batter eye line and daring them to take her on. Armed with sharp leg breaks, subtle googlies, and deceptive changes of pace, she combines classic wrist-spin romance with the fierce competitive edge required in the cutthroat environment of modern white-ball cricket.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>Her path to the top was built on patience and hard work in domestic cricket. Starting out with Lahore Women as a teenager, she initially found it challenging to establish herself, but persistent technical refinement began to pay off. Turning out for the Higher Education Commission and the PCB Blasters, she produced a breakthrough performance in the final of the 2019 PCB Triangular One Day Women Tournament. Her crucial wickets of international stars Bismah Maroof and Javeria Rauf steered PCB Blasters to the championship title and marked her out as a special talent to watch.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>After serving as a travelling reserve at the 2022 Women Cricket World Cup in New Zealand, Tuba was handed her senior international T20 debut on May 24, 2022 against Sri Lanka in Karachi. What followed was one of the greatest fairy-tale debuts in cricket history. Bowling with<br />\r\nstunning precision and bite, she recorded figures of three wickets for eight runs in four overs, registering the best bowling figures by a Pakistani woman on T20 debut. She finished the series with five wickets at an average of 8.80 and was named the ICC Women Player of the Month for May 2022, becoming the first female cricketer from Pakistan ever to receive that prestigious global honor.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>Tuba rapid ascent continued as she represented Pakistan at the 2022 Commonwealth Games in Birmingham and later made her ODI debut against Australia at the North Sydney Oval in January 2023. She also contributed valuably with the bat down the order, proving she could hit<br />\r\nboundaries under pressure as evidenced by an enterprising knock of 28 against England in the 2023 T20 World Cup. In a squad often dominated by finger spinners, her wrist-spin provided captain and team management with an indispensable wicket-taking option during middle overs, capable of breaking stubborn partnerships out of nowhere.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>During 2024 and 2025, Tuba expanded her tactical repertoire and strengthened her consistency. She was an essential part of Pakistan squad at the 2024 ICC Women T20 World Cup in the UAE, while also dominating the domestic circuit for the Strikers in both T20 and One Day<br />\r\nCups. In 2025, she delivered decisive all-round displays in domestic tournaments, including quick-fire cameos and economical spells that regularly turned games. Her fielding inside the circle and her boundary catching also developed into major assets, giving Pakistan an electric<br />\r\nathletic presence on the park.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>The year 2026 saw Tuba in sparkling form across multiple international assignments. She was in fine rhythm during the home T20 series against Zimbabwe in May 2026, picking up wickets while conceding runs at barely four per over, and followed it up with spirited performances during the 2026 Ireland Tri-Nation series and the bilateral tour of Sri Lanka. In September 2026 at the Women Asia Cup T20 in Dubai, she produced a match-winning cameo with both bat and ball against Thailand, smashing 12 runs off four deliveries before taking two wickets for 17 runs. With her match-winning flair and growing experience, Tuba Hassan stands as a primary weapon in Pakistan pursuit of international success.</p>","relation":null,"social_details":{"social_platform":[{"social_platform_id":"1","social_platform_name":"facebook","social_platform_url":"https://www.facebook.com/@tubahassanofficial/"},{"social_platform_id":"2","social_platform_name":"twitter","social_platform_url":"https://x.com/tubahassan_72?lang=en"},{"social_platform_id":"3","social_platform_name":"instagram","social_platform_url":"https://www.instagram.com/_tubahassan/?hl=en"},{"social_platform_id":"4","social_platform_name":"youtube","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"5","social_platform_name":"pinterest","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"6","social_platform_name":"linkedin","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"7","social_platform_name":"josh","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"8","social_platform_name":"moj","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"9","social_platform_name":"chingari","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"10","social_platform_name":"mx_takatak","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"11","social_platform_name":"tiktok","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"12","social_platform_name":"koo","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"13","social_platform_name":"clubhouse","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"14","social_platform_name":"telegram","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"15","social_platform_name":"sharechat","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"16","social_platform_name":"snapchat","social_platform_url":""}]},"trophy_cabinet":{"trophy_details":[{"id":54,"name":"ICC Player